/**
 * @page tracking_loop Tracking Loop Design
 *
 * @tableofcontents
 *
 * @section tracking_goal Goal
 *
 * The tracking loop keeps the locally generated code replica aligned with the
 * incoming signal after acquisition has produced an initial code phase and
 * Doppler estimate.
 *
 * The C++ entry point is demo::TrackingLoop::update(). The API declaration
 * intentionally contains only the caller-facing contract; this page contains
 * the longer algorithm explanation.
 *
 * @section tracking_flow Processing flow
 *
 * The diagram source lives separately in `docs/diagrams/tracking.mmd`.
 *
 * @mermaidfile tracking.mmd "Tracking signal flow"
 *
 * @section tracking_states State model
 *
 * | State | Entry condition | Main behavior |
 * |:------|:----------------|:--------------|
 * | Acquiring | Object construction | Wait for an initial acquisition result |
 * | Tracking | initialize() succeeds | Update discriminator, filter, and NCO every epoch |
 * | Lost | Lock metric falls below policy | Stop trusting loop output and request reacquisition |
 *
 * @section tracking_equations Core equations
 *
 * The normalized Early-minus-Late discriminator is:
 *
 * @f[
 *   e_k = NormalizedEarlyLate{|E_k|}{|L_k|}
 * @f]
 *
 * The simplified loop-filter update used by this example is:
 *
 * @f[
 *   LoopFilterUpdate
 * @f]
 *
 * The notation is:
 *
 * | Symbol | Meaning | Unit |
 * |:-------|:--------|:-----|
 * | @f$E_k@f$ | Early correlator magnitude | arbitrary |
 * | @f$L_k@f$ | Late correlator magnitude | arbitrary |
 * | @f$e_k@f$ | DLL discriminator output | dimensionless |
 * | @f$f_k@f$ | NCO frequency correction | Hz |
 * | @f$K_p, K_i@f$ | Loop-filter gains | implementation-defined |
 *
 * @section tracking_contract Important invariants
 *
 * - initialize() must run before the first update().
 * - Exactly one task owns a TrackingLoop instance.
 * - update() is called once per 1 ms epoch.
 * - The real-time update path performs no dynamic allocation.
 * - Loss-of-lock policy belongs above the low-level discriminator math.
 *
 * @section tracking_why Why the long explanation is outside the header
 *
 * The header should answer questions a caller needs while using the API:
 * preconditions, ownership, units, side effects, lifetime, and concurrency.
 *
 * This page answers questions a maintainer needs while changing the algorithm:
 * processing flow, state transitions, equations, and design invariants.
 */
